  "Number": 16,
  "Question": "If \\(f(x)=5x^2-2x-1,\\) then \\(f(x+h)-f(x)\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(5h^2-2h\\)",
    "B": "\\(10xh-4x+2\\)",
    "C": "\\(10xh-2x-2\\)",
    "D": "\\(h(10x+5h-2)\\)",
    "E": "\\(3h\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "To find \\(f(x+h)\\), replace every \\(x\\) in the formula by \\(x+h\\). When subtracting \\(f(x)\\), the constant terms cancel, while the linear terms contribute \\(-2h\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x+h)-f(x)\n&=5\\bigl((x+h)^2-x^2\\bigr)-2h\\\\\n&=5(2xh+h^2)-2h\\\\\n&=10xh+5h^2-2h\\\\\n&=\\boxed{h(10x+5h-2)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe second line uses \\((x+h)^2=x^2+2xh+h^2\\). This identity holds for all real \\(x,h\\), including \\(h=0\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
